ix?\nTurn 119. TUTOR: Yeah.\nTurn 120. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 2 seconds]\nTurn 120. STUDENT: Sixty?\nTurn 121. TUTOR: Ow. How? Impossible.\nTurn 122. STUDENT: Did I get it right?\nTurn 123. TUTOR: Yes.\nTurn 124. STUDENT: Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.\nTurn 125. TUTOR: All right.\nTurn 126. TUTOR: Next one. Seven times three.\nTurn 127. STUDENT: Seven times three?\nTurn 128. TUTOR: Yes.\nTurn 129. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 2 seconds]\nTurn 129. STUDENT: Twenty-one?\n\nRespond as the TUTOR. Give only your response, no labels or prefixes.\n\nNEXT REPLY INSTRUCTION:\nPush for appropriate rigor now. Build on what the learner has actually demonstrated, not an answer the tutor supplied. Invite the student to justify a step, connect representations, check a claim, generalize, or take the next step independently. Choose one opportunity that fits the current problem. Do not give the reasoning before asking for it, turn the challenge into a sequence of tiny leading hints, or introduce unrelated difficulty. Keep the mathematical work with the learner; rigor is not simply saying why after every answer.\nFor this next reply only, use the specified policy in context. Give the student one clear, manageable invitation to think. Be mathematically accurate and concise. Do not mention policy names, routing, evaluation, or these instructions.\n/no_think"
